# THE ISSUE TRIAGE DRIVE: every open issue carries a guess at its next step, kept current.
#
# One half, two strata, two rules. An issue in this repository that has moved past its last
# judgement gets a row of this package's own — the issue's facts copied onto it, and a mark of
# what was judged — and a row not yet judged is judged, by an unattended task on the coderunner
# reading the issue's text and discussion, main, and the roadmap. The judgement lands on the
# row: `next`, what the next step is, one of a small vocabulary; `arc`, whether that step runs
# with the direction of verse; `why`, one paragraph. When the issue moves — a comment, a label,
# a linked commit, a close — the judgement is stale by construction and is made again. When it
# does not, nothing runs.
#
# WHAT IT REPLACED, and why. The first drive briefed each new issue against the code, once, by
# a task a person had to claim. Two things about the ground truth made that a nice proof and a
# poor fit. Most of the activity on an issue is out of band of any step verse defines: an
# issue is written already triaged, or its author meant triage to be premature — a design note
# with a decision pending, where "which source files does this touch" is not yet a question —
# and a one-shot brief could say neither. And the brief lived in a brokered task, which is a
# layer between the person and the issue for anybody working issues their own way; 25 of the
# 29 briefs staging ever posed were declined unread. So the judgement here is a guess at the
# NEXT STEP rather than a step, "underway already, nothing to do" is one of its answers, and
# nobody claims anything: it runs unattended and lands where `verse data` reads it.
#
# THE VOCABULARY is small so that a rule can act on it later, and each word is a claim the
# judge has to back with what it read:
#
#     next: underway   — work is in progress; a linked commit, a branch, a comment says so
#     next: decide     — a question stands before any work, and `why` names it
#     next: build      — concrete against main as it stands, and `why` says where
#     next: close      — done or moot on main, and `why` says what settles it
#     next: note       — a record rather than a task; nothing to do
#
# `arc` is the same judgement about direction: `on` the roadmap's threads, `ahead` of them
# (right, and waiting on something the roadmap orders first), or `beside` them (not what verse
# is for, or not yet). `thread` names the roadmap thread it belongs to, or `none`. What comes
# AFTER a guess — a `decide` posed as a gate to the author, a `close` proposed for approval, a
# `build` handed to a fix task — is not here; the vocabulary is chosen so those are rules added
# beside this one rather than a rewrite of it.
#
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/issue-triage.tony
#
# ── SEEDING, which is one write per open issue and comes FIRST ───────────────────────────
#
# Installing over a backlog judges all of it: every open issue matches until a row says it was
# judged as it stands, and there is no predicate for "created after now" (change-events.md).
# To skip the backlog, seed a row per open issue that says it was judged at its current
# updatedAt, BEFORE installing — so only an issue that moves from then on is judged:
#
#     scripts/triage-seed.sh            # one row per open issue: {judged: {id, updatedAt}}
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/issue-triage.tony
#
# Seeding after installing is a race the install wins.
#
# ── THE IDIOM: stale by construction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A condition cannot name its subject (condread.go), so "this issue's row says it was judged at
# this issue's updatedAt" is not a read the condition can make directly. What it can do is read
# every row's `judged` — an object {id, updatedAt} — as a list, and hold the issue against the
# list: an issue matching none of them has not been judged as it now stands. `!not.or` over a
# list of object patterns is exactly that, and an empty list selects everything. The judged
# mark carries the id so that two issues moved in the same second cannot stand in for each
# other.
#
# The mark is written by the DERIVE, in the same firing that copies the issue onto the row, so
# the same issue is not marked twice for one change: the rule's own read (the rows) wakes it
# when the derive lands, and it selects nothing. That is the loop closing, not a cycle. The
# judgement itself is a PATCH on the row by the judge's completion hooks — a derive may carry
# its subject and nothing from a task's answer (deriveMayReference), which is why there are two
# rules and why the second one's subject is the row. A judgement that failed latches too, so
# an issue whose judge fell over is judged again when it moves, not on every commit until then.
at: verse-dev.triage
reads: [ !verse-path [git-issue] ]
---
# The reflected slice, where an ISSUE is the subject. Level 0 because nothing this package
# writes is upstream of it: the source writes here and this package never does.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git-issue

# One row per issue, keyed by the issue: the judgement and the mark of what was judged. It is
# this package's own slice, since the source is the single writer of `git-issue.*` and a
# judgement written onto the issue would be writing into somebody else's.
- name: next
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, triage, next]
---
# 1. An open issue in this repository that no row says was judged as it now stands gets a row:
# the issue's facts, copied — the judge's prompt binds against the payload that fired it, so
# what it reads has to be on the row — and the mark of what is being judged.
#
# The subject is the ISSUE, and that is what makes the issue's commits wake this rule. `stage`
# is removed here rather than set: it is the row's own state (the judge writes it), and a
# derive restating it would latch the row shut; removing it is what reopens the row for a
# judgement when the issue moves.
- name: mark
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [git-issue, issue, "*"]
      value: !let
        let:
        - judged: !list-paths {at: [verse-dev, triage, next, "*"], path: judged}
        in: !and
        - {repo: verse, status: open}
        - !not.or .[judged]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, triage, next]
      id: $subject.value.id
      value:
        issue: $subject.value.id
        title: $subject.value.title
        createdAt: $subject.value.createdAt
        updatedAt: $subject.value.updatedAt
        labels: $subject.value.labels
        commits: $subject.value.commits
        branches: $subject.value.branches
        related: $subject.value.related
        blocks: $subject.value.blocks
        blockedBy: $subject.value.blockedBy
        body: $subject.value.body
        discussion: $subject.value.discussion
        judged: {id: $subject.value.id, updatedAt: $subject.value.updatedAt}
        stage: !delete null
---
# 2. …and a row with no judgement yet is judged.
#
# A task's id is (rule, subject), so this is `judge@verse-dev.triage.next.<id>` with the
# causing commit as its occurrence: one run per time the row is reopened. `!not.has-path
# stage` is the latch, spelled that way for the reason the drift drive gives: a bare null is
# hidden on read, so `!not {stage: null}` comes back as `!not {}` after a restart and selects
# everything.
- name: judge
  stratum: next
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, triage, next, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path stage
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.coderunner
      # The fixing environment for its checkout of main and its git, under a grant that lets
      # it act on nothing: a judge reads and answers. Delivery is not in it, so no branch.
      environment: verse-fix
      driver: llm
      grant: read-only
      vector: auto
      # THE ANSWER IS A CONTRACT. An unparseable final message is no answer: `$data` resolves to
      # nothing, the completion hook is refused, and the judgement is lost silently. Every key
      # is a string, and the prompt says what each may hold.
      answer:
        next: !irtype string
        arc: !irtype string
        thread: !irtype string
        why: !irtype string
      prompt: |
        You are judging what the NEXT STEP is for one issue in the verse repository, which is
        checked out at ./verse at the tip of main. You are not doing the step. Read what you
        need, once, and answer.

        THE ISSUE, as its tracker holds it now:

            id:        $issue
            title:     $title
            created:   $createdAt
            updated:   $updatedAt
            labels:    $labels
            commits:   $commits
            branches:  $branches
            related:   $related
            blocks:    $blocks
            blockedBy: $blockedBy

        Its description:

        $body

        Its discussion, oldest first (`at`, `text`):

        $discussion

        WHAT YOU ANSWER. A JSON document with exactly four string keys, nothing before it and
        nothing after it, every key and every string quoted:

            {"next": "...", "arc": "...", "thread": "...", "why": "..."}

        `next` is ONE of these words, and each is a claim you have to back in `why` with what
        you read:

            underway  work on this is in progress. A linked commit, a branch, a comment that
                      says somebody is on it, a related issue that is. Nothing to do.
            decide    a question stands before any work can, and you name it. A design note
                      with a choice pending, a proposal waiting on a call, two readings of the
                      ask that lead to different work. "Which files does this touch" is NOT
                      the question when the issue is at this stage; do not answer it.
            build     the issue is concrete against main as it stands: you can say where the
                      change goes (files, Go symbols spelled the way Go does, `entity.JoinSegs`)
                      and nothing has to be decided first.
            close     main already does what the issue asks, or the thing it is about is gone,
                      or it duplicates another issue. Say what you read that settles it.
            note      a working note, a record of a decision, a place to keep an argument.
                      Not a task, and nothing to do.

        `arc` is whether that step runs with the direction of verse: `on` the roadmap's
        threads, `ahead` of them (right, but waiting on something the roadmap orders first),
        or `beside` them (not what verse is for, or not yet). Judge it against
        ./verse/docs/roadmap/README.md — its threads and its "Rough order" — and the
        "What is built, and what is not" section of ./verse/docs/README.md. `thread` is the
        roadmap thread the issue belongs to, by the heading's own words, or `none`.

        `why` is ONE paragraph: what you read and what it settles. Name what you read — a
        commit, a comment's date, a file, a heading — so a reader can check you. Do not
        summarise the issue; it already says what it says.

        READ THE TREE, NOT YOUR MEMORY OF IT. An issue outlives the code it was filed against.
        Before you say `build` or `close`, read what stands on main now. Before you say
        `underway`, say what shows it. Read each thing once; a second read of a file does not
        make a verdict truer. When every claim in your answer is backed, answer.
    onDone:
      patch:
        next: $data.next
        arc: $data.arc
        thread: $data.thread
        why: $data.why
        stage: judged
        err: !delete null
    # A judge that fell over latches too, with the failure on the row: the issue is judged
    # again when it moves, not on every commit until then. Same for a cancelled one.
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        err: $err
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: declined
        err: !delete null
